WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.290
Narrator: What makes "Scary
Stories to Tell in the Dark"

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:09.281
so, well, scary

00:00:09.281 --> 00:00:10.870
are the monsters.

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:14.050
The disfigured Jangly Man
and the bulbous Pale Lady

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:16.090
look as if they've been simply lifted

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:17.720
from the '80s children's books

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:20.520
and dropped onto the big screen.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:21.790
But getting those creatures

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:24.810
from 2D to 3D was no simple feat.

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:27.030
To do it, producer Guillermo del Toro

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:28.723
and director André Øvredal

00:00:28.723 --> 00:00:30.653
enlisted the help of Spectral Motion.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.830
Through a process of sculpting, painting,

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:36.410
and careful scrutiny
of the original images,

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:39.510
here's how the monsters for
"Scary Stories" came alive.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:48.620
This is Mike Elizalde,

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:52.120
president and creative
director of Spectral Motion.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:53.270
You've seen their work.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.650
Mike Elizalde: The film that
we're known for is "Hellboy."

00:00:56.650 --> 00:01:00.150
Since then, we've done several
other high-profile films,

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:03.770
including "X-Men," "Fantastic
Four," "Attack the Block."

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:06.310
We worked on the first
season of "Stranger Things."

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:07.800
Narrator: For "Scary Stories
to Tell in the Dark,"

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.800
del Toro picked Spectral Motion to bring

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:12.220
the book's iconic images to life.

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:14.730
Now, usually, when creating characters

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:17.240
from an existing source, like
in the case of "Hellboy,"

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.427
his team would have
hundreds of illustrations

00:01:19.427 --> 00:01:21.120
to inform their designs.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.467
But for "Scary Stories," in most cases,

00:01:23.467 --> 00:01:27.383
they had only one illustration
by artist Stephen Gammell.

00:01:27.383 --> 00:01:30.210
So, to start, Spectral enlisted
special-effects designers

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:32.210
Mike Hill and Norman Cabrera

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:34.770
to help bring the monsters to life.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:37.003
Mark Viniello: He was very
vocal about, you know,

00:01:37.003 --> 00:01:38.144
the art direction,

00:01:38.144 --> 00:01:39.420
and we would constantly send him updates.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.868
But what I recall is the first edict was

00:01:41.868 --> 00:01:44.453
they have to be as true to the
source material as possible.

00:01:44.453 --> 00:01:45.940
Like, that's bottom line.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:49.450
They have to be a representation
of that original art.

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:52.130
So, we had the pictures
everywhere as a constant reminder

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:55.200
to everybody, like, this
is what we're going for.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.040
Norman Cabrera: You have
one picture per story,

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:01.310
but they were just such great,
strong, iconic single images

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:04.880
that it was just really
important to capture that feel.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:05.790
Narrator: One of the first things

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:08.070
they had to decide was
what type of creature

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:09.750
they were going to make.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:11.707
Instead of making puppets, Spectral Motion

00:02:11.707 --> 00:02:14.200
wanted each one to be a performer,

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.520
which dictated how they
would create the character.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.620
Cabrera: We would
literally take the drawing,

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.915
the Stephen Gammell
drawing, and in Photoshop,

00:02:21.915 --> 00:02:24.720
like, lay it over to make
sure that all the lines

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.560
are right where they should
be and that sort of thing.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:30.150
Narrator: But these
characters are gonna be in 3D.

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.160
In the case of the Scarecrow design,

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.170
Norman created a digital
art version in this program,

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:37.350
ZBrush, to start designing the angles

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:39.340
not captured in the illustration.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:42.520
But the most important thing
was to nail the front shot.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.950
Once they got approval
from del Toro and Øvredal,

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:46.670
they finished a more detailed sculpt,

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:49.323
made a mold, and filled
it with foam latex.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:53.610
The pieces are designed
to fit specific actors.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:57.030
In some instances, the actor's
physicality directly informs

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.970
the look and feel of the
character they were playing.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:01.780
The Toeless Corpse, for example,

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:03.890
was played by Javier Botet,

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:07.190
who's worked with del Toro
on "Crimson Peak" and "Mama."

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:08.473
Viniello: Javier Botet is

00:03:08.473 --> 00:03:10.100
an incredible creature-suit performer.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.996
He has very unique
physical characteristics

00:03:12.996 --> 00:03:15.087
that we were able to,

00:03:15.087 --> 00:03:17.162
starting with that as our foundation,

00:03:17.162 --> 00:03:19.410
really helped in the translation

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:21.523
from a 2D drawing into something 3D.

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:25.690
Narrator: Another creature
actor, Troy James,

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:28.070
who played the Jangly
Man, brought a new level

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:29.990
of physicality that
required more attention

00:03:29.990 --> 00:03:31.780
because of the amount
of bending and twisting

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:33.590
the character had to do.

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:35.750
Viniello: We had some new ground to break,

00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:38.980
as far as having him come in
maybe a little more frequently

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:40.880
than we normally would for a creature suit

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.400
because of the unique
physicalities of the character

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:46.193
and how we had to work around that.

00:03:46.193 --> 00:03:49.500
So, it's just a matter of
having the actors available

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:50.900
to come in repeatedly.

00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:52.990
And we'll put him in the
suit and we'll take video.

00:03:52.990 --> 00:03:54.634
We'll see, OK, this is
working, this isn't.

00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:56.720
We've got to address this.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:58.070
Narrator: I want to point
out that these moves

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:00.357
are not done with wires or CGI.

00:04:00.357 --> 00:04:02.190
He can really move like that.

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:04.498
Elizalde: When we first learned about

00:04:04.498 --> 00:04:07.300
what the Jangly Man had to do,

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.895
we started scratching our
heads and asking each other,

00:04:09.895 --> 00:04:11.460
"Who's gonna play this character?"

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:14.710
And then we became aware of
an actor named Troy James,

00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:17.320
who had done a spot on
"America's Got Talent,"

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:19.492
and we took a look at
his work and we thought:

00:04:19.492 --> 00:04:22.423
"He's the guy. This guy can do anything."

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:28.330
Narrator: Once in costume,
the next stage was painting.

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:30.510
Both the suits and the performer's skin

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:32.240
were painted with acrylics.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:34.360
This stage had its own set of challenges.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:37.710
For the Pale Lady, del Toro
and Øvredal wanted it to appear

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:40.310
as if the creature's dress
had merged with her skin.

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:44.490
To do so, Mike Hill had to
create a smooth paint job

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:47.020
void of any garment lines on her skin.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:48.190
Cabrera: It was really cool.

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:50.500
I know Guillermo was
particularly happy with this one,

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:52.906
because it was such a large and,

00:04:52.906 --> 00:04:55.210
not abstract, but, you know,
it's one of those things

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:57.030
that could be widely interpreted,

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:58.350
like, what you're looking at.

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:01.600
Stephen's drawings, they
have a sketchy vibe to them,

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.728
so not every line is
realized, so to speak,

00:05:04.728 --> 00:05:07.000
you know, in the real world.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:07.920
Narrator: The Pale Lady costume

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:10.710
had to be put on almost
like a giant snowsuit.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.240
Once in, to create the seamless look,

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:15.980
they glued up the seams
and painted over them.

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:18.560
Now, keep in mind, these are
black-and-white drawings.

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:20.460
For a character like the Toeless Corpse,

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:21.510
they chose a color palette

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:23.932
to give her a "nicotine-stained" feel,

00:05:23.932 --> 00:05:26.520
using rotted browns
and earth-toned colors.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:27.990
They also had to tackle the challenge

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:30.663
of giving this character's
face a skeletal look.

00:05:31.640 --> 00:05:33.670
To do so, they added pronounced teeth

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:35.680
outside the actor's mouth,

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.610
a technique used for zombies
on "The Walking Dead."

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:40.390
They painted a majority
of the actor's body

00:05:40.390 --> 00:05:43.660
and covered him with almost
30 individual applications

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:45.300
to create the finished look.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:47.267
But the one thing crucial to the design

00:05:47.267 --> 00:05:48.842
required a missing toe.

00:05:48.842 --> 00:05:50.080
So Spectral designed a foot

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:52.547
that would be finished
later in post-production.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.490
The hair for those two
were custom-made wigs

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:00.070
by Lynne Watson.

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:02.900
She's done costume hair work
for "Planet of the Apes,"

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:05.450
"Lone Ranger," and my personal favorite,

00:06:05.450 --> 00:06:07.920
"Team America: World Police."

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:10.060
Each wig took over three weeks to make

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:12.183
and are both made with human hair.

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:14.417
When designing the Scarecrow,

00:06:14.417 --> 00:06:16.175
Cabrera and the team asked:

00:06:16.175 --> 00:06:18.397
What would farmers in
the world of this movie

00:06:18.397 --> 00:06:20.813
make a scarecrow out of, and how well

00:06:20.813 --> 00:06:22.446
would they have built it?

00:06:22.446 --> 00:06:23.810
They decided to give the Scarecrow

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:26.690
a look as though it had
a 2-by-4 skeletal frame

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:28.973
and was completely beaten
up by the elements.

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:32.110
For the Jangly Man,

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:34.760
the character made up of
different limbs and body parts,

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:36.240
each segment of the creature

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:38.400
would therefore have
different levels of rot.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:42.280
So, although several designers
all worked on this one suit,

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:45.100
they would each work on
various limbs separately,

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:47.910
focusing on a unique level of rot.

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:49.860
Del Toro would visit and guide the artists

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:51.840
on how rotted certain parts would be

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:53.090
and how they should look.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:55.780
Once they were in the suit, painted up,

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:57.090
detailed, and ready to shoot,

00:06:57.090 --> 00:06:58.780
the final step was getting the movement

00:06:58.780 --> 00:06:59.980
of the characters right.

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:03.730
Along with the direction
of Øvredal and del Toro,

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:06.500
production hired motion coaches
to develop the performance.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:09.400
The actor, director, and
even the creature designer

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:11.330
would all work together
to give the monster

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:14.410
that perfectly terrifying
presence on camera.

00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:16.432
Cabrera: It is like a Dr.
Frankenstein kind of thing,

00:07:16.432 --> 00:07:18.473
you know? Like, you're making a monster.

00:07:18.473 --> 00:07:20.160
This thing has to live.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:21.700
And even though it's
not living in real life,

00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:23.500
it's living in a movie.

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:25.800
So, you're taking this from nothing

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:28.290
that didn't live before,
and now it's alive.

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:30.331
And that's, like, a great feeling

00:07:30.331 --> 00:07:32.050
when you finally see it on film

00:07:32.050 --> 00:07:34.600
and it's this living, breathing
thing that's convincing

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:37.260
'cause it's in the dramatic
context of a movie.

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:41.044
And that's the best reward there is.

